  "note": "Run on the live AwwO, in the operator’s own account and workspace, on 2026-10-26, every agent on Claude Opus 5.5 working in its own sandbox computer. The film answers an Opus 5.5 video prompt by Eian (x.com/EianLu/status/2111662397278847478, prompt 12760 of YouMind’s Opus 5.5 collection): a data film of a city’s growth over 3,271 years pressed into 149 seconds, every building from open data. AwwO’s version is the growth of AwwO itself: its public repository from the first public commit (5 September 2026) to 25 October 2026, 51 days and 789 commits, drawn as a city whose ground is what the first commit already held and whose districts are AwwO’s own code. The data in the page is the operator’s read of the public repository’s history on 2026-10-26 (for every day with commits: the commits and each area’s files and bytes), and every number on screen comes from it. The canvas’s text was written for this film by the operator beforehand: the Director’s task (the prompt adapted to AwwO, with the facts the film may state and the look), the kernel contract every module writes to, each builder’s duty, and the tester’s and the editor’s tasks. What is seen and heard is what the canvas’s agents wrote. The canvas ran six times, each run after the first running only the nodes it named, on the kept outputs of the rest. The first (10:59 to 11:27 UTC) finished everything but the edit: the editor wrote no timeline, because its plan (each frame at the tester’s slowest time and a half, 180 s a piece) made a timeline of 1.94 MB, over the edit’s 1.9 MB, and its own checks stopped it. The second (12:05 to 12:08) ran the editor again with a task that plans at the tester’s mean time and a quarter and 300 s a piece, and the third (12:18 to 12:43) the edit, which delivered a film. Looked at before publishing, it was a flat white tower on a dark ground, then a small pale cluster far off: the operator’s direction had asked for buildings “pale, lit softly from within” and a camera pulling back as the city grows. The direction was rewritten (each district’s buildings solid, in its own colour and lit, their heights on a log scale, the camera keeping the city at least half the frame’s height, the closing title below the city), and the fourth run (13:19 to 13:43) ran the Director and everything after it but the edit again from it; the fifth (14:06 to 14:49), started from the canvas’s page, ran the edit. Before publishing, the data turned out to hold each day’s last commit subject, one of which named a media generator; the subjects, which the film never draws, were dropped, and the sixth run (15:03 to 15:46) ran the assembly again with that data, then the tester, the editor, which cut the page into sixteen pieces of 4 s, and the edit, which rendered each piece from the page and recorded the page’s own sound (window.renderAudio), with no model: the film here. Its tester found that the Buildings module read each district’s files under keys the data does not have, so no building was ever built (the fourth run’s tester had found and fixed the same), and fixed it with one line, and made the opening ground a little lighter so that the first two seconds count as drawn; the page then passed 29 of its 30 tests and caught all 4 planted failures, the one failing being the self-test’s own time in a browser without a GPU (92 s against 60). The canvas’s Review node ran beside the edit and is not part of this case.",
